<h2><span class="n">2.6</span> Router Operation Review — the desk's #1 lever, on the stand</h2>

<p class="lead">Garrath — you asked for the works: what the router did all week, what paid, what erred, whether the thresholds and the new untradeable-meter need tuning, and whether the whole machine is earning its keep. Here it is, money-first. <b>Lead with what survived:</b> (1) the new untradeable-day meter is the standout — it scored the two green days lowest (13, 23) and Friday's −$905 bloodbath highest (86), a near-perfect line-up with the day's actual P&amp;L; (2) the <b>durable</b> 5-min router held its nerve — across its two live days it made just <b>2</b> autonomous switch-changes (both graded GOOD) and correctly sat frozen through all of Friday's untradeable chop, <b>zero real blocked-loss leakage</b>; (3) the desk finished the week <b>net green +$625</b> (Mon–Fri +$852) even after eating that Friday. What still bites: the router keeps <b>over-benching aligned momentum in a trend</b> (the recurring BAD-call), and Friday's damage came almost entirely from the operator-directed <b>enable-all-at-the-open</b>, not from the routine ticks. One robustness-gated Saturday knob survives the anti-overfit filter (fader-bench ER floor 0.20&rarr;0.15); everything else is hold-and-re-validate.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="ct">Honesty banner up front: this is <b>one week, ~5 trading days, one summer RANGE regime</b>, and the threshold sweep is in-sample on thin, partly-already-gated realised trades. Every backtest below is segmented (fader-set vs full-set, per-regime leak buckets) — no blanket cross-tape number is presented as truth. Treat single-week $ as directional, not proven. Re-validate after a real TREND week before deploying anything.</div>
</div>

