<p><b>Read:</b> the thrash dropped hard once the durable tick took over routine ticking — 07-30 (hand-managed) churned the switches 35 times in a day; 07-31 (durable) made <b>zero</b> autonomous routine changes and let the operator drive the exceptions. The ledger scoreboard stands at <b>GOOD 17 · BAD 2 · CHURN 4 · NEUTRAL 2</b> — the two BAD calls and all four CHURN calls are the same two lessons repeating (premature-arm on a delta-blip; benching the aligned trend-rider on open-noise). More on those next.</p>
</div>

<!-- ============================ SECTION 2 ============================ -->
<div class="card">
<h3>2 · What works vs what doesn't</h3>
<p>Walking the graded ledger, the pattern is clean: the router <b>saves money defensively</b> (benching faders/counter-trend on chop and roll-over days, shadow-confirmed) and <b>errs offensively</b> (benching aligned momentum too eagerly, or arming it too eagerly on a blip). Money attached where measurable:</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Grade</th><th class="num">$ impact</th><th>counterfactual / proof</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">07-30 14:51 bench both momentum longs on ER-collapse regime flip</td><td><span class="tag">✅ GOOD</span></td><td class="num">≈ +$400 saved</td><td>unbenched momentum shadows lost −$398 over that window (thrust_aligned −202, cont −118, loose −78)</td></tr>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">07-31 defensive trim &rarr; fully-flat through the ATR-52/62 open violence</td><td><span class="tag">✅ GOOD</span></td><td class="num">≈ +$500 saved</td><td>every benched gate's shadow bled (grind_fast −153, thrust_loose −153, exhaustion_rev −99, abs_veto −80)</td></tr>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">07-30 14:57 arm short-side fade book for the roll-over chop</td><td><span class="tag">✅ GOOD</span></td><td class="num">+$372</td><td>exhaustion_short went 4/4 live fading the roll-over — best offense of the day</td></tr>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">07-31 held flat through the low-vol overnight grind-up (+141pt)</td><td><span class="tag">✅ GOOD</span></td><td class="num">churn avoided</td><td>all 8 earlier gate-fires stopped — a low-vol grind is untradeable, flat was right</td></tr>
<tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">07-30 15:49 FLIP to longs on a delta-blip in ER-0.01 chop</td><td><span class="tag">❌ BAD</span></td><td class="num">process-loss</td><td>faded in 2 min; salvaged to ~+$55 by operator Claims but the read was wrong &rarr; banked the "ER-climb not delta-blip" rule</td></tr>
<tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">07-30 13:36 bench grind on the open flush (aligned trend intact)</td><td><span class="tag">❌ BAD</span></td><td class="num">≈ −$430 missed</td><td>operator overrode; grind then rode to new highs +$234/+$197 &rarr; banked "don't bench the trend-rider at the open"</td></tr>
<tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">07-30 grind toggled ~8&times; through the morning</td><td><span class="tag">⚠ CHURN</span></td><td class="num">≈ $0 (thrashy)</td><td>grind's 0.35 ER floor + abs_veto's veto meant most premature arms never fired — process error, not $ error</td></tr>
<tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">07-31 afternoon abs_veto re-enables into the choppy recovery (&times;2)</td><td><span class="tag">⚠ CHURN</span></td><td class="num">≈ −$245</td><td>even the veto-protected, scalp-exit abs_veto churns a choppy recovery — the 55s veto is a microstructure filter, not a regime filter</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<div class="callout"><div class="ct"><b>The one durable truth from the whole ledger:</b> the router's cheap error is a wrongly-benched gate (missed trades); its expensive error is a wrongly-armed one (real churn). Every ❌/⚠ this week was an <b>arming</b> mistake or a <b>bench-the-trend</b> mistake — never a "left a bleeder armed" mistake. The defense works; the offense over-manages.</div></div>
</div>

