</table>

<p><b>Honest reading (this number is noisier than it looks):</b> the raw net is −$1,216, but two things inflate the "missed" column — the dual A/B slots double-count one signal, and the giant "missed" figures on 07-29 (−$1,717 value) are dominated by <b>grind_long shadow winning during brief benched windows on a trend day</b>. That is the exact recurring error surfacing quantitatively: <b>the leak is over-benching aligned momentum in a trend</b>, and it is opportunity-cost (winners not taken), <b>not</b> realised bleed. The proof: on both durable days (30/31) real leakage was <b>$0</b> — the router let no live loser through. So:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Not-bleeding job: PASS.</b> Zero real blocked-loss leakage on the days the durable router actually ran; +$807 of genuine defense on 07-30.</li>
<li><b>Catching-winners job: LEAKY.</b> It benches momentum too readily during trends (07-29's −$1,717 "value", 07-30's ❌ open-flush bench). The leak is concentrated in <b>momentum gates during TREND_UP windows</b> — the exact segment the "don't bench the trend-rider" rule exists to protect.</li>
</ul>
<p class="lead" style="margin-top:8px">For context, the exit-width <b>selector</b> (a separate machine) captured only <b>$1,995 of a possible $7,614 (26%)</b> this week, leaving $5,619 regret — worst on 07-30 (chose tight, missed the wide runners: −$2,748 regret). That is a bigger leak than the router and belongs in the selector's own review, flagged here so it isn't mistaken for router value.</p>
</div>

