<div class="card">
<h3>4 · Threshold tuning — the sweep</h3>
<p>Ran <span class="ln">router_study.py --days 40</span>: the ER-trend &times; net-min threshold grid plus the timing sweep (step/hold/window/fast_exit), on <b>both</b> the live-managed fader set and the full historical fader set. Router Δ vs the no-router baseline (shipped timing), higher = better:</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>ER floor \ NET min</th><th class="num">20</th><th class="num">30 *shipped</th><th class="num">40</th><th class="num">50</th><th class="num">60</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">ER 0.15 (looser trend def)</td><td class="num">+461 / +1300</td><td class="num">+492 / +1307</td><td class="num">+428 / +1294</td><td class="num">+316 / +1120</td><td class="num">+158 / +858</td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">ER 0.20 *current</td><td class="num">+224 / +1044</td><td class="num"><b>+176 / +995</b></td><td class="num">+152 / +976</td><td class="num">+152 / +1014</td><td class="num">+152 / +816</td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">ER 0.25</td><td class="num">+152 / +787</td><td class="num">+152 / +787</td><td class="num">+152 / +806</td><td class="num">+152 / +806</td><td class="num">+152 / +744</td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">ER 0.30</td><td class="num">+152 / +334</td><td class="num">+152 / +334</td><td class="num">+152 / +334</td><td class="num">+152 / +334</td><td class="num">+152 / +428</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p style="font-size:0.9em"><i>(each cell = live-managed set Δ / full historical set Δ, in $)</i></p>

<p><b>What both sets agree on (the robust signal):</b> the <b>ER 0.15 row beats the ER 0.20 row in every single NET column, on both universes.</b> Loosening the router's <b>trend-detection floor</b> from 0.20&rarr;0.15 lets it call "trend" sooner and therefore bench counter-trend faders more — and in this fader-heavy, counter-trend-loss-dominated sample that is worth roughly <b>+$300 (live set) / +$300 (full set)</b> over shipped. That is the one change that survives the two-sets-must-agree filter.</p>

<div class="callout"><div class="ct"><b>Anti-overfit discipline — what I am NOT recommending, and why:</b><br>
• The grid's <b>joint-best</b> (ER0.15 / NET20 / window45 / step5 / hold3) scores +$692 live / +$984 full but sits on the <b>loosest grid edge</b> on three axes at once, helps only 4–6 of the sample days, and <b>step=5m</b> would triple the tick-rate = a thrash-machine on a live desk. Rejected as overfit.<br>
• <b>NET-min stays at 30.</b> On the live set ER0.15 actually scores <i>higher</i> at NET30 (+492) than NET20 (+461); on the full set they tie (+1307 vs +1300). No reason to loosen it.<br>
• <b>Timing stays shipped</b> (window 30m, step 15m, hold 2, fast_exit off). The timing gains are grid-edge and thin-n.<br>
• The <b>grind 0.35 ER ARM floor</b> is a <i>different</i> threshold (momentum-arming, not fader-benching) and is validated separately (+$728 over 10d) — leave it. <b>Day-bias &plusmn;40</b> — no evidence to move it. Leave it.</div></div>
</div>

