<p><b>Roundtrip + give-back carry the whole meter; stops is weak supporting context; ATR is noise</b> — exactly as the design note claimed. The 40/40/20 blend is right to lean on the two that work. <b>The 65 cutoff wants no change</b> — it caught the −$905 with room to spare and produced zero false STAY-OUTs on green days. One refinement worth a look: both CAUTION days (47, 56) still lost money, so CAUTION isn't costless — consider making CAUTION an <b>action</b> (faders-off / veto-gates-only / half-size) rather than just a label. n=2, so propose-and-validate, don't hard-wire.</p>
</div>

<!-- ============================ SECTION 6 ============================ -->
<div class="card">
<h3>6 · Bench-decision review — grading the week's actual benches</h3>
<p>The Friday US-open sequence is the teaching case. Operator said "enable all gates, US OPEN" and left; the open printed a real up-thrust (ER 0.68, break 28713) that then <b>fully reversed</b> (−172pt roundtrip). Here is exactly how the six gates behaved, live:</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Gate armed at the open</th><th>type</th><th class="num">open P&amp;L</th><th>lesson</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">abs_veto (55s-veto protected)</td><td>VETO-gate</td><td class="num">+$146</td><td>caught the thrust, banked both legs, held disciplined</td></tr>
<tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">grind (no veto)</td><td>CHURNER</td><td class="num">−$370</td><td>bought the rollover top, 4 stops in 90s</td></tr>
<tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">exhaustion_short (off-regime)</td><td>FADER</td><td class="num">−$72</td><td>fired in ATR-62 violence — a 0.5R scalp can't cover a 62pt stop</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<div class="callout"><div class="ct"><b>Banked lesson [[us-open-arm-veto-gates-not-churners]]:</b> at a violent open, arm the <b>veto-protected</b> gates (abs_veto) and keep the <b>churners</b> (grind) and <b>off-regime faders</b> benched. Enable-all guarantees the wrong-side gates churn the reversal before anyone can bench them. The defensive trim that followed (grind&rarr;capit/rgv&rarr;exhaustion&rarr;fully-flat) was <b>shadow-vindicated</b>: every benched gate's shadow bled through the ATR-52/62 chop.</div></div>

<p><b>The individual bench grades:</b></p>
<ul>
<li><b>grind benches</b> — right when the trend <i>structure</i> breaks (07-31 open reversal, 07-30 14:51 ER-collapse: both GOOD); wrong when the trend is intact and it's just open-noise (07-30 13:36: BAD). The distinction is roundtrip + ER-collapse, and the meter now measures exactly that.</li>
<li><b>abs_veto scalp-then-bench</b> — the fire-and-stop exception to its veto protection worked (07-30 22:10 bench after it stopped both legs = GOOD). But re-enabling it into a <b>choppy recovery</b> (07-31 pm, ×2, −$245) was CHURN: the 55s veto is a microstructure filter, so a chop micro-thrust passes it. Rule holds: don't re-arm even abs_veto until a genuinely clean trend (ER≥0.35 <i>sustained</i>).</li>
<li><b>the exhaustion_short live trial</b> — deployed the 0.5R/1.5R fade-scalp; it fired <b>once, off-regime</b>, in the open violence (net −$72, as predicted for high ATR). The core thesis (fader-scalp in <i>normal</i> chop, ATR 11–18) is <b>still untested</b> — one off-regime fire is not a verdict. Keep it pinned and running into more low/mid-ATR roll-over afternoons before judging.</li>
<li><b>the fully-flat "stay-out" calls</b> — the best calls of Friday. Once the day was read untradeable, going flat banked <b>zero further bleed</b>; the meter held 74–96 STAY-OUT the whole time confirming it. Stay-out is a real, earned decision, not a cop-out.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================ SECTION 7 ============================ -->
<div class="card">
<h3>7 · Durable router &amp; architecture — is polling the right mechanism?</h3>
<p><b>Did the durable 5-min systemd tick manage correctly?</b> Yes. It is <span class="tag">active / enabled</span>, fires on schedule, and across its two live days it made <b>2</b> autonomous switch-changes (07-30 22:10 bench abs_veto after fire+stop, 22:15 bench the 3 shorts + grind under a fresh UP bias — <b>both graded GOOD</b>) and then correctly logged "changed: none" through the entire Friday untradeable day. <b>No thrash, no naked positions, no double-writes, zero real leakage.</b> The robustness fix works: the desk was never unmanaged.</p>

<p><b>Is polling the best mechanism, though?</b> Here is the honest cost of the 5-min poll. The router only acts on the tick boundary, so when a regime flips mid-window, up to 5 minutes of churn can print before the switch changes. The clearest single instance this week: on the Friday open, grind bought the reversal and lost <b>−$370 over ~4 minutes</b> before it was benched. A per-gate <b>in-code regime-guard</b> (an entry-time veto that blocks a counter-regime fill in milliseconds, no polling) would have stopped most of that at the source.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>reaction lag</th><th class="num">addressable leak this week</th><th>trade-off</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="ln">5-min poll (current durable)</td><td>up to 5 min</td><td class="num">baseline</td><td>simple, fail-safe, survives session exit; laggy on fast flips</td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">event-watcher (in-chat, current)</td><td>~seconds</td><td class="num">partial</td><td>already cuts lag on breaks/bleed — but dies with the session</td></tr>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">in-code per-gate regime-guard (proposed)</td><td>&lt;1 sec, at entry</td><td class="num">up to −$956 / −$1,933</td><td>catches the counter-trend NEAR-MISS bucket the poll+coarse-threshold lets through</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><b>Where that leak number comes from (regime-segmented, per the discipline):</b> the study's trade classification buckets the fader trades by regime-context. The <b>"counter-trend near-miss"</b> bucket — trades that entered against a detectable regime and lost — is <b>n=15 / −$956 (live set)</b> and <b>n=32 / −$1,933 (full set)</b>. That is the leak a millisecond in-code guard could address that a 5-min poll structurally cannot. <b>Recommendation:</b> keep the poll as the fail-safe floor, but scope an in-code counter-regime entry-veto on the momentum gates as the next build — it attacks the exact segment (counter-trend fills in a live trend) where polling loses. Flagged as a build, not a Saturday switch.</p>
</div>

<!-- ============================ SECTION 8 ============================ -->
<div class="card">
<h3>8 · Verdict &amp; Saturday recommendations</h3>
<p class="lead">Is the router earning its keep? <b>Yes — as a defense.</b> Zero real blocked-loss leakage on the days it ran durably, +$807 of shadow-confirmed defense on 07-30, a new untradeable-meter that called all five days correctly, and a durable tick that held flat through the worst day of the week without a session driving it. The desk finished <b>net green (+$625 incl Sunday, +$852 Mon–Fri)</b> through a week that contained a −$905 day. The weakness is offensive: it over-benches aligned momentum in trends (opportunity-cost, not bleed) and the exit-selector left $5.6k of regret on the table (separate machine, separate review).</p>

<table>
